    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nWhen we subtract a positive number from another positive number, we move towards the left on the number line.\n\nContent:\nFor example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3 - 5]\n\nContent:\nTo subtract [MATH: 5] from [MATH: 3,] we start at [MATH: 3] on the number line and move [MATH: 5] places to the left.\n\nGraphic:\n[IMG: /graphics/56b655e3f8d8b98343920f0ac1014b72]\n\nContent:\nWhen we do this, we land at [MATH: -2.] Therefore,\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 3-5 = -2.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Subtracting a Positive Number From a Non-Negative Number\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: 0 - 4?]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo subtract [MATH: 4] from [MATH: 0,] we start at [MATH: 0] on the number line and move [MATH: 4] places to the left.\n\n[IMG: /graphics/a497a5e56dcbd612069cab3f37e379a4]\n\nWhen we do this, we land at [MATH: -4.] Therefore, [MATH: 0-4 = -4.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 0 \u2212 8 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 8]\nb. [MATH: 0]\nc. [MATH: 8]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 (1)/(8)]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(8)]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 3 \u2212 8 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 7 \u2212 12 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Tutorial: Subtraction With Larger Numbers\n\nContent:\nIt is impractical to use number lines to subtract large numbers.\n\nContent:\nHowever, a neat trick makes subtracting a positive number from a smaller positive number very easy!\n\nContent:\nAs an example, let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}}]\n\nContent:\nSince [MATH: 68] is larger than [MATH: 37,] the answer must be negative.\n\nContent:\nWe start by solving an easier problem: subtracting the numbers in the reverse order. In our case, the reverse problem is\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}}.]\n\nContent:\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 6|8; \u2212|3|7; 3|1]\n\nContent:\nSo, we have\n\nContent:\n[MATH: {\\color{blue}{68}} - {\\color{red}{37}} = 31.]\n\nContent:\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above.\n\nContent:\nTherefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: {\\color{red}{37}} - {\\color{blue}{68}} = -31.]\n\nContent:\nAnd that's it!\n\nContent:\nLet's use the same idea to subtract two three-digit numbers.\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Example: Subtracting a Positive Number From a Non-Negative Number With Larger Numbers\n\nExample Prompt:\nFind the value of [MATH: 170 - 281.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nSince [MATH: 281] is larger than [MATH: 170,] the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n[MATH: 281 - 170]\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n[MATH: 2|8|1; \u2212|1|7|0; 1|1|1]\n\nSo, we have\n[MATH: 281-170 = 111.]\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n[MATH: 170 - 281 = -111.]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 12 \u2212 36 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 69 \u2212 103 =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 36]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 36]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 34]\nd. [MATH: 34]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 44]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 206 \u2212 364 =][FREE_ENTRY 1]"
